<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_pressure" target="ai_capital_governance::secondary_market_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising perceived escalation risk increases risk aversion and liquidity premia, reducing IPO/activity and lowering trading volume and aftermarket liquidity for AI‑related securities.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_capital_governance</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_pressure" target="china_tech_trade_rivalry::trade_controls_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising escalation pressure in the Russia–Ukraine war prompts Western governments to broaden and tighten export controls on dual‑use and advanced technologies to prevent diversion to Russia, and those controls are pragmatically extended to China or implemented in ways that raise barriers for Chinese tech firms.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">china_tech_trade_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_pressure" target="iran_hormuz_crisis::perceived_escalation_ris" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher measured escalation pressure in the Russia–Ukraine theater lowers perceived thresholds for reciprocal military action and normalizes rapid escalation dynamics, causing regional actors and external stakeholders to assess a higher likelihood of wider escalation in the Hormuz confrontation.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_crisis</data>
</edge>
