<edge source="alliance_cred_apac::external_military_provoc" target="ai_capital_governance::capital_flow_to_ai" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Spikes in external military provocations prompt governments to announce emergency funding and procurement for surveillance/defense AI and push private investors toward security‑oriented AI startups, rapidly increasing capital directed into AI development.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">alliance_cred_apac</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_capital_governance</data>
</edge>
<edge source="alliance_cred_apac::external_military_provoc" target="china_tech_trade_rivalry::trade_controls_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">A spike in hostile incidents directed at alliance members produces immediate political pressure for punitive economic measures and coordinated export controls or sanctions against the perceived adversary, increasing overall trade-control intensity.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">alliance_cred_apac</data>
  <data key="d39">china_tech_trade_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="alliance_cred_apac::external_military_provoc" target="china_tech_trade_rivalry::state_export_support_int" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Sustained external military pressure on allied economies incentivizes governments to accelerate subsidies, financing and market-support programs to bolster domestic firms and reduce vulnerability to coercive measures.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">alliance_cred_apac</data>
  <data key="d39">china_tech_trade_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="alliance_cred_apac::external_military_provoc" target="iran_hormuz_crisis::maritime_incident_freq" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.9</data>
  <data key="d36">An uptick in hostile or coercive military actions from outside the alliance (strikes, ship harassment, seizures) directly produces more attacks, interdictions, and close‑encounter incidents against commercial and naval vessels in the maritime domain.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">alliance_cred_apac</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_crisis</data>
</edge>
<edge source="alliance_cred_apac::external_military_provoc" target="iran_hormuz_crisis::perceived_escalation_ris" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.9</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising external military provocations increase stakeholders' assessments that the confrontation may broaden or intensify by creating pressure for reciprocal responses and signaling willingness to escalate.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">alliance_cred_apac</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_hormuz_crisis</data>
</edge>
<edge source="alliance_cred_apac::external_military_provoc" target="russia_ukraine_war::escalation_pressure" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">An uptick in hostile/coercive military incidents by external actors in APAC reduces Western political bandwidth and increases perceptions of a globally contested security environment, incentivizing adversaries in the Russia–Ukraine war to intensify operations before allied support is further constrained, raising escalation pressure.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">alliance_cred_apac</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
